// VIDEO dh008 — "What if every "6-7" got twice as loud?"  (tone FUNNY: a meme what-if ladder, one consequence per
// place). Leo whispers "6-7" in Room 4 at 30 dB; every repeat after it is +10 dB (≈ twice as loud to the ear), and nobody can stop
// saying it back: the coffee ripples, the class, the whole school at recess (even the seesaw), a dad sets off every car alarm, a
// stadium chants it past the pain threshold and the LED wall cracks, downtown's windows shatter, a seismograph draws "67" and the
// scientist reads it out loud, a parrot on a power line says it and every bird faints, and past 194 dB it is a shockwave around
// Earth. +1 day: the planet agrees never again… until Ms. Diaz says "turn to page six—" and the class answers. The Moon blows up.
// Then the Sun.
// Kids are shown kindly; the teacher's despair is cartoon (hands on head, mouth 'o'); birds faint (X eyes) and are fine.
// No real people: Leo, Ms. Diaz, the dad, the scientist and the parrot are ours; the song behind the meme is never quoted.
//
// Sources (checked Oct 29 2026; where the physics is approximate the screen says ≈ / "roughly"):
//  - "6-7" ("six seven"): Gen Alpha meme from 2025 — the phrase + a two-palms up/down seesaw gesture; traced to the 2024 song
//    "Doot Doot (6 7)" by Skrilla, spread by basketball clips (LaMelo Ball, 6'7") and a viral clip of a kid shouting it; teachers
//    banned it or turned it into lessons — Dictionary.com named "67" its 2025 Word of the Year (Oct 28, 2025; finalists incl.
//    "agentic", "aura farming"): Dictionary.com via Scripps News / WKBW (Oct 28, 2025); Education Week (Oct 29, 2025);
//    Today.com (Oct 2025)
//  - +10 dB ≈ "twice as loud" to the ear (a psychoacoustic rule of thumb, loudness doubles per +10 phon): HyperPhysics (GSU),
//    "Loudness"; so the 18th one is 2^17 = 131,072× the first (HUD "×" lines are this arithmetic)
//  - reference levels (approximate, they vary by source and distance): whisper ≈ 30 dB, conversation ≈ 60 dB, vacuum ≈ 70 dB,
//    lawnmower ≈ 90 dB, rock concert ≈ 110 dB, pain threshold ≈ 120–130 dB, jet engine at 30 m ≈ 140 dB: NIDCD "Common sounds"
//    (UNT web archive); Caltrans "Typical noise levels"; Honeywell noise thermometer
//  - glass: windows fail at ≈ 0.15–0.5 psi overpressure; 0.5 psi ≈ 165 dB SPL (our conversion: 20·log10(3,447 Pa / 20 µPa)), so
//    "≈ 160 dB (roughly)": safteng.net blast-overpressure table; Acoustical Society of America (Starship booms, 2024)
//  - Krakatoa, Aug 27 1883: a barometer 160 km (100 mi) away recorded a spike ≈ 172 dB; heard ≈ 4,800 km (3,000 mi) away on
//    Rodrigues; the pressure wave circled Earth several times: Nautilus, Aatish Bhatia "The sound so loud that it circled the
//    Earth four times" (2014)
//  - ≈ 194 dB = the loudest undistorted sound in air at sea level (the trough reaches vacuum); louder = a shockwave: Nautilus (same)
//
// Shot list (seconds)                                    | set    | what happens
//  0.0– 5.4  clsA   (title over it) "6-7" #0             | class  | Room 4 from the back: Ms. Diaz chalks PAGE 67; a paper airplane bonks the board
//  5.4– 8.8  clsB   #1 30 dB                             | class  | close on Leo: he whispers it, two-palm seesaw; the kids behind grin
//  8.8–13.2  cof    #2 40 · #3 50                        | class  | macro on the coffee mug: rings in the coffee; then the mug hops and the coffee leaps
// 13.2–18.6  clsC   #4 60 · #5 70                        | class  | reverse on 24 faces: the class does it in sync; papers blizzard, the clock drops, Ms. Diaz: hands on head
// 18.6–21.4  parkA  #6 80                                | park   | recess: the whole school does the hands at the lens; the swings kick up
// 21.4–24.2  parkB  #7 90                                | park   | the seesaw does the gesture too; the swings loop over the bar, the merry-go-round spins up, leaves fly
// 24.2–26.8  burbA  #8 100                               | burb   | a dad washing his car can't help it: "…six seven."
// 26.8–29.4  burbB                                       | burb   | every car on the street hops with its alarm blinking; mailbox flags pop up
// 29.4–32.8  stadA  #9 110 · #10 120                     | stadium| 16,000 fans chant it with their arms, the bowl jumps
// 32.8–35.8  stadB  #11 130                              | stadium| the LED wall shows "6-7", cracks, sparks, goes black
// 35.8–38.6  strA   #12 140 · #13 150                    | street | a sidewalk crowd chants it; pigeons scatter, the hot dogs pop
// 38.6–41.2  strB   #14 160                              | street | down the street, every shop window shatters in a wave toward the lens
// 41.2–43.8  labA                                        | lab    | the seismograph monitor's trace writes "67"
// 43.8–46.6  labB   #15 170                              | lab    | the scientist reads it out loud → blown back in his chair, papers, the drum spins off
// 46.6–49.0  birdA  #16 180                              | burb   | a parrot on a power line among the songbirds: "SIX SEVEN!" — every bird faints, swings upside down
// 49.0–51.6  birdB                                       | burb   | birds rain onto the lawns feet-up around a neighbor
// 51.6–57.0  space  #17 190 · #18 200                    | space  | Earth from orbit: a giant bubble over the town; a ring; past 194 dB a gold shockwave sweeps the planet
// 57.0–60.6  endA   +1 DAY → 0 dB                        | class  | everyone, hands over mouths; Ms. Diaz, relieved: "Okay! Turn to page six—" … she freezes
// 60.6–62.4  endB   #19 210                              | class  | the class lowers its hands: "…SEVEN." (the biggest jolt)
// 62.4–64.0  space2                                      | space  | the biggest ring wraps Earth and hits the Moon: BOOM! — fire, rock chunks, a shock ring
// 64.0–70.0  sunCam                                      | space  | the ring keeps going and reaches the Sun: it swells, flashes white, explodes. Fade 65.4, card 66.0
